1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,811
Here are two wheels.

2
00:00:01,811 --> 00:00:05,781
They have exactly the same mass, and exactly the same radius.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,676
The only difference: in the ring, all the mass sits out at the rim.

4
00:00:10,676 --> 00:00:15,889
In the disc, the same mass is spread evenly, all the way to the centre.

5
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:22,034
We release both from rest, at the top of identical slopes.

6
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:24,365
Which one reaches the bottom first?

7
00:00:24,365 --> 00:00:25,817
Make a guess before you watch.

8
00:00:29,533 --> 00:00:30,863
The disc wins.

9
00:00:30,863 --> 00:00:32,178
Every single time.

10
00:00:32,178 --> 00:00:36,084
Not because it is heavier, and not because it is smaller.

11
00:00:36,084 --> 00:00:38,376
Same mass, same size.

12
00:00:38,376 --> 00:00:42,738
The secret is how the mass is arranged around the axis of spin.

13
00:00:42,738 --> 00:00:46,718
By the end of this lesson, you will be able to prove exactly why.

14
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:56,639
Welcome to Class 12 Physics, Unit 1: Rotational Dynamics.

15
00:00:56,639 --> 00:01:02,759
Everything you learned about straight line motion in Class 11 has a twin in the world of spin.

16
00:01:02,759 --> 00:01:06,486
Learn the translation, and this whole unit writes itself.

17
00:01:08,867 --> 00:01:10,119
Here is our path.

18
00:01:10,119 --> 00:01:19,952
Angular kinematics, rotational kinetic energy, moment of inertia, the inertia of a rod, torque, work and power, and finally, angular momentum.

19
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:29,054
Picture a rigid body spinning about a fixed axis through the point O.

20
00:01:29,054 --> 00:01:33,306
Every particle of the body moves in a circle around that axis.

21
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:37,532
Look at three particles at different distances from the axis.

22
00:01:37,532 --> 00:01:41,477
The outer one travels a longer arc than the inner one.

23
00:01:41,477 --> 00:01:44,199
But every one of them turns through the same angle.

24
00:01:44,199 --> 00:01:50,597
That shared angle, theta, is why angle, not distance, is the natural language of rotation.

25
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:56,210
We measure that angle in radians: the arc length s, divided by the radius r.

26
00:01:56,433 --> 00:01:57,737
What is one radian?

27
00:01:57,737 --> 00:02:01,734
Take the radius itself, and bend it onto the circle.

28
00:02:01,734 --> 00:02:06,940
The angle it covers is exactly one radian, about fifty seven degrees.

29
00:02:07,167 --> 00:02:13,380
Keep laying radii around the rim: two, three, four, five, six, and a little bit more.

30
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:18,028
Exactly two pi radii fit around a full circle.

31
00:02:18,028 --> 00:02:21,185
So one revolution is two pi radians.

32
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:30,737
How fast the angle changes is the angular velocity, omega, equal to d theta by d t, in radians per second.

33
00:02:30,967 --> 00:02:40,618
And how fast omega itself changes is the angular acceleration, alpha, equal to d omega by d t, in radians per second squared.

34
00:02:42,833 --> 00:02:46,311
Here is the key that unlocks the whole chapter.

35
00:02:46,311 --> 00:02:49,488
Every linear quantity has a rotational partner.

36
00:02:49,488 --> 00:02:51,884
Displacement becomes angle.

37
00:02:51,884 --> 00:02:54,788
Velocity becomes angular velocity.

38
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:58,004
Acceleration becomes angular acceleration.

39
00:02:58,004 --> 00:03:00,413
Mass becomes moment of inertia.

40
00:03:00,413 --> 00:03:01,949
Force becomes torque.

41
00:03:01,949 --> 00:03:04,267
And momentum becomes angular momentum.

42
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:11,565
Remember this table, and you can rebuild almost every formula in this unit, just by swapping the symbols.

43
00:03:12,767 --> 00:03:13,784
Watch it work.

44
00:03:13,784 --> 00:03:18,955
These are the three equations of motion you already know for constant acceleration.

45
00:03:19,167 --> 00:03:25,301
Swap u and v for omega nought and omega, a for alpha, and s for theta.

46
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:30,732
Out come the three equations of angular motion, valid whenever alpha is constant.

47
00:03:30,933 --> 00:03:35,887
Even the formula for the angle turned in the n-th second carries straight across.

48
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:39,354
Now connect the two worlds.

49
00:03:39,354 --> 00:03:47,050
Since s equals r theta, and r is fixed for a particle, differentiating gives v equals r omega.

50
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:53,748
Every particle shares the same omega, so its speed grows in proportion to its distance from the axis.

51
00:03:54,967 --> 00:03:59,851
If the spin is speeding up, a point on the rim has two accelerations.

52
00:03:59,851 --> 00:04:04,264
The tangential acceleration, r alpha, changes its speed.

53
00:04:04,467 --> 00:04:13,292
And the centripetal acceleration, v squared over r, or r omega squared, points to the centre and keeps turning its direction.

54
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:20,941
They are at right angles, so the total acceleration is the square root of a t squared plus a c squared.

55
00:04:22,767 --> 00:04:25,946
Here is the trap that costs marks every year.

56
00:04:25,946 --> 00:04:34,683
v equals r omega, s equals r theta, and torque equals I alpha are true only when angles are in radians.

57
00:04:34,683 --> 00:04:38,615
If a question gives revolutions per minute, convert first.

58
00:04:38,615 --> 00:04:48,042
One revolution is two pi radians, so N revolutions per minute means omega equals two pi N over sixty radians per second.

59
00:04:48,833 --> 00:04:50,540
Let us try a real problem.

60
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:59,629
A grinding wheel starts from rest and reaches three hundred revolutions per minute in ten seconds, with uniform angular acceleration.

61
00:04:59,629 --> 00:05:07,665
Find the angular acceleration, the number of revolutions it makes, and the final speed of a point forty centimetres from the axis.

62
00:05:15,467 --> 00:05:16,732
First, convert.

63
00:05:16,732 --> 00:05:26,267
Three hundred revolutions per minute is two pi times three hundred, over sixty, which is ten pi, about thirty one point four radians per second.

64
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:35,840
Then alpha is the change in omega divided by time: ten pi over ten, which is pi, about three point one four radians per second squared.

65
00:05:36,067 --> 00:05:44,780
Starting from rest, theta equals half alpha t squared: half times pi times one hundred, which is fifty pi radians.

66
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:50,522
Divide by two pi for each turn, and you get exactly twenty five revolutions.

67
00:05:50,522 --> 00:05:52,136
Check the counter on the wheel.

68
00:05:52,136 --> 00:05:52,874
Twenty five.

69
00:05:53,100 --> 00:06:01,749
Finally, v equals r omega: zero point four times thirty one point four, about twelve point six metres per second at that point.

70
00:06:06,933 --> 00:06:11,414
A spinning body has energy, even though it is not going anywhere.

71
00:06:11,414 --> 00:06:12,859
Where is that energy?

72
00:06:12,859 --> 00:06:23,610
Think of the body as a crowd of tiny particles, masses m one, m two, m three, at distances r one, r two, r three from the axis.

73
00:06:23,833 --> 00:06:29,150
All the particles share the same omega, so particle i moves with speed r i omega.

74
00:06:29,367 --> 00:06:34,918
The total kinetic energy is just the sum of half m v squared for every particle.

75
00:06:35,133 --> 00:06:42,067
Substitute r i omega for v i. Omega is the same for everyone, so pull it out of the sum.

76
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:43,434
Look at the bracket.

77
00:06:43,434 --> 00:06:48,018
The sum of m r squared contains only masses and distances.

78
00:06:48,018 --> 00:06:52,171
It depends on the body and the axis, not on the motion at all.

79
00:06:52,171 --> 00:06:54,931
We give it a name: the moment of inertia, I.

80
00:06:56,133 --> 00:07:01,122
So the rotational kinetic energy is one half I omega squared.

81
00:07:01,122 --> 00:07:04,273
Put it beside one half m v squared.

82
00:07:04,273 --> 00:07:10,454
Moment of inertia does for rotation exactly what mass does for straight line motion.

83
00:07:10,667 --> 00:07:19,028
And a wheel that rolls has both kinds at once: translational energy for its forward motion, plus rotational energy for its spin.

84
00:07:19,028 --> 00:07:20,252
Hold on to that.

85
00:07:20,252 --> 00:07:21,834
It is the key to our race.

86
00:07:26,633 --> 00:07:35,476
The moment of inertia of a body about an axis is the sum of each mass times the square of its perpendicular distance from that axis.

87
00:07:35,476 --> 00:07:40,567
For a continuous body, the sum becomes an integral of r squared d m.

88
00:07:40,567 --> 00:07:42,902
Its unit is kilogram metre squared.

89
00:07:44,133 --> 00:07:46,244
Why does distance matter so much?

90
00:07:46,244 --> 00:07:49,890
Here are two identical rods with identical masses.

91
00:07:49,890 --> 00:07:53,471
On the left, the masses sit close to the axis.

92
00:07:53,471 --> 00:07:56,622
On the right, the same masses sit far out.

93
00:07:56,622 --> 00:07:58,408
We give both the same twist.

94
00:07:58,633 --> 00:08:00,770
The left one spins up quickly.

95
00:08:00,770 --> 00:08:02,619
The right one barely gets going.

96
00:08:02,619 --> 00:08:11,185
Its masses are about three times further out, and because the distance is squared, it has about eleven times the moment of inertia.

97
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:18,092
So unlike mass, moment of inertia is not one fixed number for a body.

98
00:08:18,092 --> 00:08:26,035
It depends on three things: the total mass, how that mass is distributed, and which axis you choose to spin it about.

99
00:08:27,267 --> 00:08:30,875
Here are the six results the syllabus expects you to know.

100
00:08:30,875 --> 00:08:38,597
A thin rod: one twelfth M L squared about its centre, one third M L squared about one end.

101
00:08:38,597 --> 00:08:41,084
A ring: M R squared.

102
00:08:41,084 --> 00:08:45,420
A disc or solid cylinder: one half M R squared.

103
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:49,144
A solid sphere: two fifths M R squared.

104
00:08:49,144 --> 00:08:52,836
And a hollow sphere: two thirds M R squared.

105
00:08:53,067 --> 00:08:54,214
Notice the pattern.

106
00:08:54,214 --> 00:08:59,045
The more of the mass that sits far from the axis, the bigger the number in front.

107
00:08:59,045 --> 00:09:03,099
The ring, with everything at the rim, has the largest of all.

108
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:07,687
Now a neat idea: the radius of gyration.

109
00:09:07,687 --> 00:09:11,606
A disc has its mass spread from the centre to the rim.

110
00:09:11,606 --> 00:09:19,284
Could we squeeze all of it into one thin ring, at just the right radius, without changing the moment of inertia?

111
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:20,348
Yes.

112
00:09:20,348 --> 00:09:24,775
That radius is called k, the radius of gyration.

113
00:09:24,775 --> 00:09:30,158
For a disc it comes out at R over root two, about seventy one percent of the way to the rim.

114
00:09:30,367 --> 00:09:36,645
By definition, I equals M k squared, so k equals the square root of I over M.

115
00:09:36,645 --> 00:09:42,837
It is the distance at which the whole mass, concentrated, would give the same moment of inertia.

116
00:09:47,067 --> 00:09:50,011
Two theorems save you a lot of integration.

117
00:09:50,011 --> 00:10:01,722
The parallel axis theorem: if you know I about an axis through the centre of mass, the moment of inertia about any parallel axis, a distance d away, is I c m plus M d squared.

118
00:10:01,933 --> 00:10:13,193
And the perpendicular axis theorem, for flat sheets only: the moment of inertia about an axis perpendicular to the sheet equals the sum about two perpendicular axes lying in its plane.

119
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:16,568
Now we can settle the race.

120
00:10:16,568 --> 00:10:22,831
Both wheels start at the same height, so both convert the same potential energy, M g h.

121
00:10:22,831 --> 00:10:27,041
But a rolling wheel must split that energy between moving forward and spinning.

122
00:10:27,267 --> 00:10:36,266
For the ring, I is M R squared, and with omega equal to v over R, its spin energy equals its forward energy.

123
00:10:36,266 --> 00:10:39,624
Half of all the energy goes into spinning.

124
00:10:39,833 --> 00:10:48,650
For the disc, I is only half M R squared, so spin takes just one third of the energy, and two thirds is left for moving forward.

125
00:10:48,867 --> 00:10:54,233
More energy for forward motion means more speed at every point on the slope.

126
00:10:54,233 --> 00:10:56,850
That is why the disc always wins.

127
00:10:56,850 --> 00:11:02,383
Mass far from the axis makes a body lazy to spin, and that laziness costs speed.

128
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,300
One more example.

129
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:14,349
A solid disc flywheel of mass twenty kilograms and radius half a metre spins at one hundred and twenty revolutions per minute.

130
00:11:14,349 --> 00:11:17,178
Find I, k, and its kinetic energy.

131
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:26,013
For a disc, I is half M R squared: half times twenty times zero point two five, which is two point five kilogram metre squared.

132
00:11:26,233 --> 00:11:34,464
The radius of gyration is the square root of two point five over twenty, about zero point three five four metres.

133
00:11:34,464 --> 00:11:36,888
That is R over root two, just as we found.

134
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:44,194
Convert the speed: one hundred and twenty r p m is four pi, about twelve point five seven radians per second.

135
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:51,356
And the energy is half I omega squared: about one hundred and ninety seven joules, stored in the spin.

136
00:11:56,533 --> 00:12:01,678
Now let us derive the moment of inertia of a uniform rod ourselves.

137
00:12:01,678 --> 00:12:04,777
This derivation is a favourite long question.

138
00:12:04,777 --> 00:12:12,485
Take a thin rod of mass M and length L, with the axis through its centre C, perpendicular to the rod.

139
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:21,400
The mass per unit length is M over L. Pick a tiny element of length d x, at a distance x from the centre.

140
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:31,656
Its mass is d m equals M over L times d x, and since it is so small, its moment of inertia is simply x squared d m.

141
00:12:32,867 --> 00:12:36,749
Each element contributes in proportion to x squared.

142
00:12:36,749 --> 00:12:39,796
Let us plot that contribution along the rod.

143
00:12:39,796 --> 00:12:43,155
Elements near the centre add almost nothing.

144
00:12:43,155 --> 00:12:45,888
Elements near the ends add the most.

145
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:52,755
Adding up all the elements from minus L by two to plus L by two is exactly an integral.

146
00:12:52,755 --> 00:12:58,185
The moment of inertia is the shaded area under this curve, times M over L.

147
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:04,178
Integrate x squared to get x cubed over three.

148
00:13:04,178 --> 00:13:10,310
Put in the limits: each end gives L cubed over eight, and the two halves add.

149
00:13:10,310 --> 00:13:16,656
Simplify, and the moment of inertia about the centre is M L squared over twelve.

150
00:13:17,867 --> 00:13:20,343
Now move the axis to one end.

151
00:13:20,343 --> 00:13:25,977
The same rod, but every element is now further from the axis, so the area must grow.

152
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:32,998
Integrating from zero to L gives M L squared over three, four times bigger.

153
00:13:32,998 --> 00:13:48,558
And we can check it with the parallel axis theorem: the end is L by two from the centre, so I c plus M times L by two, squared, is M L squared over twelve plus M L squared over four, which is M L squared over three.

154
00:13:48,558 --> 00:13:49,320
It matches.

155
00:13:52,233 --> 00:13:54,969
Quick check, straight from past papers.

156
00:13:54,969 --> 00:13:58,198
A uniform rod, axis through one end.

157
00:13:58,198 --> 00:13:59,044
Which is it?

158
00:13:59,044 --> 00:13:59,920
Pause and think.

159
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:10,343
It is B, M L squared over three.

160
00:14:10,343 --> 00:14:12,570
One twelfth is about the centre.

161
00:14:12,570 --> 00:14:17,466
Moving the axis to the end adds M times L by two squared.

162
00:14:21,800 --> 00:14:26,580
Why is a door handle always fixed at the edge, far from the hinge?

163
00:14:26,580 --> 00:14:31,612
Let us look at a door from above, and push it in different places with the same force.

164
00:14:31,833 --> 00:14:34,645
Push near the hinge, and the door barely turns.

165
00:14:35,667 --> 00:14:40,499
Push at the edge with the same force, and it swings open easily.

166
00:14:40,499 --> 00:14:45,891
The turning effect of a force depends on where you apply it, not only on how hard you push.

167
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:49,555
Now the general picture.

168
00:14:49,555 --> 00:14:52,316
A body can turn about an axis through O.

169
00:14:52,316 --> 00:14:57,133
A force F acts at a point whose position from the axis is the vector r.

170
00:14:57,133 --> 00:14:59,748
The angle between r and F is phi.

171
00:14:59,967 --> 00:15:02,260
Split the force into two parts.

172
00:15:02,260 --> 00:15:06,987
The part along r just pulls on the axis, and does nothing to turn the body.

173
00:15:06,987 --> 00:15:10,616
Only the perpendicular part, F sine phi, turns it.

174
00:15:10,833 --> 00:15:13,035
So the torque is r times F sine phi.

175
00:15:13,267 --> 00:15:16,172
Read it another way: F times r sine phi.

176
00:15:16,172 --> 00:15:21,615
And r sine phi is the perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action of the force.

177
00:15:21,615 --> 00:15:22,629
We call it the moment arm.

178
00:15:22,833 --> 00:15:25,269
Watch what happens as the angle changes.

179
00:15:25,269 --> 00:15:30,439
At ninety degrees the moment arm is longest and the torque is greatest.

180
00:15:30,439 --> 00:15:34,697
Push straight along r, and the moment arm shrinks to zero.

181
00:15:34,697 --> 00:15:35,432
No torque at all.

182
00:15:35,633 --> 00:15:40,624
As a vector, torque is r cross F, and its unit is the newton metre.

183
00:15:41,833 --> 00:15:44,621
Now the most important result of the unit.

184
00:15:44,621 --> 00:15:58,488
Take one particle of the body, mass m i, at distance r i. Newton's second law says the tangential force on it is m i times its tangential acceleration, which is m i r i alpha.

185
00:15:58,488 --> 00:16:02,497
Every particle of a rigid body shares the same alpha.

186
00:16:02,700 --> 00:16:07,519
The torque of that force is the force times r i, so m i r i squared alpha.

187
00:16:07,733 --> 00:16:10,495
Add up the torques on all the particles.

188
00:16:10,495 --> 00:16:16,775
The internal forces cancel in pairs by Newton's third law, and alpha comes out of the sum.

189
00:16:16,1000 --> 00:16:18,030
And there it is.

190
00:16:18,030 --> 00:16:19,606
Torque equals I alpha.

191
00:16:19,606 --> 00:16:30,960
The rotational form of F equals m a. Force becomes torque, mass becomes moment of inertia, acceleration becomes angular acceleration.

192
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:32,599
Our dictionary, once again.

193
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:36,536
Here is a beautiful consequence.

194
00:16:36,536 --> 00:16:44,830
A uniform rod, mass two kilograms, length one metre, is hinged at one end and held horizontal.

195
00:16:44,830 --> 00:16:46,953
A coin rests on its free end.

196
00:16:46,953 --> 00:16:47,981
We let go.

197
00:16:47,981 --> 00:16:50,418
Does the coin stay on the rod as it swings down?

198
00:16:53,633 --> 00:16:54,793
Watch closely.

199
00:16:54,793 --> 00:16:59,416
The rod's free end drops faster than the coin, which is in free fall.

200
00:16:59,416 --> 00:17:02,775
The coin is left behind, floating above the rod.

201
00:17:02,775 --> 00:17:05,744
How can anything fall faster than free fall?

202
00:17:05,744 --> 00:17:06,618
Let us calculate.

203
00:17:08,133 --> 00:17:12,444
The weight acts at the centre of the rod, half a metre from the hinge.

204
00:17:12,444 --> 00:17:18,188
So the torque is M g times L by two, which is nine point eight newton metres.

205
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:26,023
About the end, the moment of inertia is M L squared over three, which is zero point six six seven.

206
00:17:26,233 --> 00:17:35,910
Torque equals I alpha, so alpha is nine point eight divided by zero point six six seven: fourteen point seven radians per second squared.

207
00:17:36,133 --> 00:17:44,780
The free end is one metre from the hinge, so its acceleration is L alpha: fourteen point seven metres per second squared.

208
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:46,434
That is greater than g.

209
00:17:46,434 --> 00:17:52,878
The end of the rod really does accelerate faster than a freely falling coin, so the coin is left behind.

210
00:17:58,167 --> 00:18:01,020
When a torque turns a body, it does work.

211
00:18:01,020 --> 00:18:08,873
A tangential force F at the rim, distance r from the axis, pushes through a small arc d s equal to r d theta.

212
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:11,289
Work is force times distance.

213
00:18:11,289 --> 00:18:16,068
Write F as tau over r, and d s as r d theta.

214
00:18:16,068 --> 00:18:20,729
The r's cancel, leaving d W equals tau d theta.

215
00:18:20,729 --> 00:18:27,350
For a constant torque, W equals tau theta: torque times angle, just like force times distance.

216
00:18:27,567 --> 00:18:29,951
Power is the rate of doing work.

217
00:18:29,951 --> 00:18:36,778
Divide by d t: power equals tau omega, the exact twin of P equals F v.

218
00:18:37,1000 --> 00:18:49,069
And because torque equals I alpha, the total work done equals the change in rotational kinetic energy: half I omega two squared minus half I omega one squared.

219
00:18:49,069 --> 00:18:51,789
This is the work energy theorem for rotation.

220
00:18:52,1000 --> 00:18:55,280
Let us try it on a rice mill in Jhapa.

221
00:18:55,280 --> 00:19:03,119
Its motor delivers a constant torque of twenty five newton metres while running at fifteen hundred revolutions per minute.

222
00:19:03,119 --> 00:19:06,011
Find its power, and the work it does in one minute.

223
00:19:06,233 --> 00:19:07,524
Radians first.

224
00:19:07,524 --> 00:19:13,898
Fifteen hundred r p m is fifty pi, about one hundred fifty seven radians per second.

225
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:23,916
Power equals tau omega: twenty five times one fifty seven point one, about three thousand nine hundred twenty seven watts, nearly four kilowatts.

226
00:19:24,133 --> 00:19:31,569
In sixty seconds, the work done is power times time: about two point three six times ten to the five joules.

227
00:19:36,767 --> 00:19:40,076
The last piece of the dictionary: angular momentum.

228
00:19:40,076 --> 00:19:48,435
For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis, L equals I omega, the twin of p equals m v.

229
00:19:48,435 --> 00:19:52,011
For a single particle, it is r times p times sine phi.

230
00:19:52,233 --> 00:19:53,406
Differentiate it.

231
00:19:53,406 --> 00:19:58,276
The rate of change of angular momentum is I alpha, which is the torque.

232
00:19:58,276 --> 00:20:04,008
So torque is the rate of change of angular momentum, just as force is the rate of change of momentum.

233
00:20:04,233 --> 00:20:07,750
And here is one of the great conservation laws of physics.

234
00:20:07,750 --> 00:20:13,753
If no external torque acts, d L by d t is zero, so L stays constant.

235
00:20:13,753 --> 00:20:17,099
I one omega one equals I two omega two.

236
00:20:17,099 --> 00:20:21,883
If a body makes its moment of inertia smaller, it must spin faster.

237
00:20:23,100 --> 00:20:28,675
Watch a skater, seen from above, spinning with arms stretched out and a weight in each hand.

238
00:20:28,675 --> 00:20:33,399
Out here, the moment of inertia is large, and the spin is slow.

239
00:20:33,667 --> 00:20:35,335
Now she pulls her arms in.

240
00:20:35,335 --> 00:20:40,192
No outside torque acts, so angular momentum must stay the same.

241
00:20:40,192 --> 00:20:44,853
Her moment of inertia drops, so her angular velocity shoots up.

242
00:20:44,853 --> 00:20:46,051
Look at the bars.

243
00:20:46,051 --> 00:20:47,197
I falls.

244
00:20:47,197 --> 00:20:48,708
Omega rises.

245
00:20:48,708 --> 00:20:49,949
L does not change at all.

246
00:20:50,233 --> 00:20:51,667
But look at the last bar.

247
00:20:51,667 --> 00:20:53,802
Her kinetic energy went up!

248
00:20:53,802 --> 00:20:59,336
Kinetic energy is L squared over two I, and I got smaller.

249
00:20:59,336 --> 00:21:04,063
Angular momentum is conserved, but kinetic energy is not.

250
00:21:04,063 --> 00:21:08,881
The extra energy comes from the work her muscles do, pulling the weights inward.

251
00:21:09,167 --> 00:21:12,804
Stretch the arms out again, and she slows right back down.

252
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:15,534
Let us put numbers on it.

253
00:21:15,534 --> 00:21:19,336
A student sits on a frictionless stool, holding weights.

254
00:21:19,336 --> 00:21:24,753
The moment of inertia is six, and the stool turns at two radians per second.

255
00:21:24,753 --> 00:21:27,500
Pulling the arms in drops I to two.

256
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:31,871
Find the new angular velocity and the change in kinetic energy.

257
00:21:32,100 --> 00:21:40,409
Conserve angular momentum: six times two equals two times omega two, so omega two is six radians per second.

258
00:21:40,409 --> 00:21:41,682
Three times faster.

259
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:46,967
The energy before is half times six times two squared, twelve joules.

260
00:21:46,967 --> 00:21:51,477
After, half times two times six squared, thirty six joules.

261
00:21:51,700 --> 00:21:57,477
The kinetic energy rose by twenty four joules, all supplied by the student's own muscles.

262
00:21:59,100 --> 00:22:01,211
You can see this law everywhere.

263
00:22:01,211 --> 00:22:08,698
A diver tucks in tight to somersault quickly, then stretches out to slow the spin before entering the water.

264
00:22:08,698 --> 00:22:17,083
A planet moves faster when it is closer to the Sun, because gravity pulls straight towards the Sun and exerts no torque.

265
00:22:17,083 --> 00:22:18,750
That is Kepler's second law.

266
00:22:18,750 --> 00:22:25,273
And a spinning top resists falling over, because tipping it would change the direction of its angular momentum.

267
00:22:27,700 --> 00:22:30,254
Try this one from the five mark questions.

268
00:22:30,254 --> 00:22:34,498
A spinning disc is suddenly coupled to a stationary one.

269
00:22:34,498 --> 00:22:37,038
What is the common angular velocity?

270
00:22:37,038 --> 00:22:37,898
Pause and think.

271
00:22:44,767 --> 00:22:47,138
B, four radians per second.

272
00:22:47,138 --> 00:22:55,511
No external torque, so angular momentum is conserved: two times ten equals two plus three, times omega.

273
00:22:55,511 --> 00:22:58,1000
Omega is twenty over five, which is four.

274
00:22:58,1000 --> 00:23:03,506
And kinetic energy falls from one hundred joules to forty.

275
00:23:03,506 --> 00:23:08,048
The missing sixty joules become heat in the friction between the discs.

276
00:23:10,233 --> 00:23:11,393
Let us recap.

277
00:23:11,393 --> 00:23:15,690
The equations of angular motion mirror the linear ones.

278
00:23:15,690 --> 00:23:21,146
v equals r omega links the two worlds, in radians only.

279
00:23:21,146 --> 00:23:27,852
Rotational energy is half I omega squared, where I is the sum of m r squared.

280
00:23:27,852 --> 00:23:34,115
A rod has M L squared over twelve about its centre and over three about its end.

281
00:23:34,115 --> 00:23:37,826
Torque is r F sine phi, and equals I alpha.

282
00:23:37,826 --> 00:23:41,419
Work is tau theta and power is tau omega.

283
00:23:41,419 --> 00:23:47,290
And angular momentum, I omega, is conserved when no external torque acts.

284
00:23:51,467 --> 00:23:53,539
That is rotational dynamics.

285
00:23:53,539 --> 00:23:57,680
Next time, we set things swinging, with periodic motion.

286
00:23:57,680 --> 00:24:01,684
Keep practising, and see you in the next lesson on NEBians.

